  "subtitle": "A factory from the ground up",
  "summary": "Factorio is Wube Software's factory-building game, whose version 1.0 arrived in August 2020 after years of public development. An engineer begins on an unfamiliar planet by gathering resources and crafting basic equipment. Furnaces, drills, belts and inserters gradually take over that work. Each new production chain supplies parts for more advanced machines and research. The factory grows through the player's decisions about where materials come from, how they travel and which tasks should become automatic next. Cooperative play lets several engineers share the construction.",
  "body": [
    "Factorio is Wube Software's factory-building game, whose version 1.0 arrived in August 2020 after years of public development. An engineer begins on an unfamiliar planet by gathering resources and crafting basic equipment. Furnaces, drills, belts and inserters gradually take over that work. Each new production chain supplies parts for more advanced machines and research. The factory grows through the player's decisions about where materials come from, how they travel and which tasks should become automatic next. Cooperative play lets several engineers share the construction.",
    "A stopped machine often points to a problem elsewhere. Iron plates may be arriving too slowly, a belt may be full or an assembly line may be consuming components faster than another can make them. Watching materials move provides the information needed to repair the system. Adding a furnace helps only when enough ore and power reach it. Expansion becomes a series of connected decisions, with a change at one end of the factory altering the needs of the rest.",
    "Research unlocks more ways to move resources, generate electricity and organise construction. Railways connect distant deposits, while robots can handle tasks beyond the first hand-built lines. Growing industry also creates pollution and attracts attacks from the planet's creatures. Defences must therefore share materials and power with production. The player continually balances expansion against the infrastructure supporting it. Mods and the map editor extend the same interest in systems, allowing new scenarios or substantial changes to the factory's rules."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A factory from the ground up",
    "Following the bottleneck",
    "A network to defend"
  ],
